Package: SII
Type: Package
Title: Calculate ANSI/ASA S3.5-1997 (R2024) Speech Intelligibility Index
Version: 1.1.9
Date: 2026-08-15
Authors@R: c(person(given = c("Gregory", "R."), family = "Warnes", role = c("aut", "cph")),
             person(given = "Mark", family = "Shaver", role = c("cre", "aut"), email = "mark.shaver@posteo.net"))
Description:
   Calculates the American National Standards Institute (ANSI)
   S3.5-1997 Speech Intelligibility Index (SII) (ANSI 1997,
   <https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/asa/asaansis31997r2024>), a standard method
   for computing the intelligibility of speech from acoustical
   measurements of speech, noise, and hearing thresholds. This package
   includes data frames corresponding to Tables 1 - 4 in the ANSI
   standard as well as functions utilizing these tables and user-provided
   hearing threshold and noise level measurements to compute the SII
   score. The methods implemented here extend the standard computations
   to allow calculation of SII when the measured frequencies do not
   match those required by the standard by applying interpolation.
   Furthermore, the package now includes a hybrid nonlinear loudness 
   engine integrating the excitation patterns of Chen et al. (2011) 
   with the compressive integrals of Moore & Glasberg (2004) to predict
   impaired loudness recruitment in sones. It also includes advanced 
   clinical methods to predict aided SII based on hearing aid 
   prescriptive rationales, including Open-NL (Open Non-Linear), a 
   novel theoretical rationale inspired by NAL-NL2 (National Acoustic 
   Laboratories Non-Linear 2) and DSL (Desired Sensation Level), as 
   well as functions to calculate real-ear insertion gains, estimate 
   maximum power output (SSPL90 or Saturation Sound Pressure Level for a
   90 dB input), and prescribe dynamic range compression. NOTE: This
   package and its interactive tools are strictly for non-clinical
   academic research and educational purposes. They are not approved
   for direct patient care or use as a medical diagnostic device.
   Development of this package was originally funded by the Center for
   Bioscience Education and Technology (CBET) of the Rochester
   Institute of Technology (RIT).
